# Keep docs updated

When a change meaningfully alters how the project works, **update the relevant docs in the same change** — do not leave AGENTS/README/architecture months behind the code.

## When to update

Trigger docs updates if you (or the user) introduce or change any of:

- apps, packages, modules, or major folders
- features, user-facing behavior, or data flow
- stack, commands, env vars, Docker, or workspace scripts
- agent conventions (rules, hooks, skills, lint/format expectations)

Skip for pure refactors, typo fixes, or edits that do not change structure, behavior, or contributor/agent guidance.

## What to touch (as applicable)

| Doc | Update when… |
| --- | --- |
| [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md) | stack, commands, env, Cursor layout, agent entrypoints |
| [docs/architecture.md](../../docs/architecture.md) | layers, data flow, directory map, new modules |
| [README.md](../../README.md) | product overview, features, screenshots, setup |
| [docs/README.md](../../docs/README.md) | release index / doc map |
| [docs/plans/](../../docs/plans/) | close or revise plans that the work completed or invalidated |
| `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` | conventions the agent must follow changed |

Prefer editing existing docs over adding new ones. Prefer accurate short updates over large rewrites.

## How

1. After the code change is clear, ask: “Would an agent reading only AGENTS + architecture be wrong or incomplete?”
2. If yes, patch those files (and README / plans / rules as needed) before finishing.
3. Do not invent features or APIs in docs — document what landed.
4. Release notes / screenshot folders under `docs/releases/` only when publishing a version (see docs/README.md).
